   "h": "Suspected PE scheme (Blackbook)",
   "html": "<div class=\"flow\" data-flow=\"b066a\"></div><div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Well's Criteria for PE</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clinical Signs and Symptoms of DVT (leg swelling and pain with palpation of the deep veins)</td><td>(3.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Alternative diagnosis less likely than PE</td><td>(3.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Heart rate &gt;100bpm</td><td>(1.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Immobilization or surgery in last 4 weeks</td><td>(1.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Previous DVT or PE</td><td>(1.5)</td></tr><tr><td>Hemoptysis</td><td>(1.0)</td></tr><tr><td>Malignancy (ongoing or previous 6 months)</td><td>(1.0)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p><small>Wells P.S, et al. (2000). Derivation of a simple clinical model to categorize patients probability of pulmonary embolism: increasing the models utility with the SimpliRED D-dimer. Thromb Haemost 2003; 83: 416-20.</small></p><p><small>Writing Group for the Christopher Study Investigators. (2006). Effectiveness of managing suspected pulmonary embolism using an algorithm combining clinical probability, D-Dimer testing, and computer tomography. JAMA;295: 172-179.</small></p>"
  }
